                                    <h2><span class="shaft"><img id="492-0" src="//cdn.recordinghacks.com/images/icons/patterns/icon_c.png" width=27 height=27 alt="Cardioid" title="Cardioid" ></span>Violet Design Ltd. Flamingo Junior</h2>
                    
                    <h2 class="sub">                    Cardioid Condenser Microphone</h2>
                    <p>The Flamingo Junior Standard, aka &#8220;The Junior,&#8221; and the Flamingo Junior Vintage, are downsized, cardioid only, FET versions of the company&#8217;s high-end <a href="http://recordinghacks.com/microphones/Violet/Flamingo-Standard">Flamingo tube microphones</a>.</p><p><img src="//cdn.recordinghacks.com/images/mic_extras/violet/flamingo_jr_vintage.jpg" width="150" class="full shaft" />The Junior Standard and Junior Vintage differ in only two respects:</p><ol><li>The Vintage is tuned to more closely resemble the sound of vintage mics, with warmer mids and diminished highs, and </li><li>the body is painted matte black instead of polished violet.</li></ol><p>The hardware for both mics is identical. The capsule employs a single, 26mm (large) diaphragm, shockmounted within a head described as &#8220;acoustically transparent;&#8221; it consists of multiple layers of brass mesh. Electronics are Class A and include a custom-wound humbucking transformer (as in the tube versions).</p><blockquote><p><cite>Violet</cite></p><p>The custom-tuned frequency response has smooth enhancement at the lowest registers, mid-range at 2kHz-3kHz, and also in the 12kHz region, yielding recordings with low-end punch and high-end air.</p></blockquote><p>Both models are phantom powered.<p>The <a href="//recordinghacks.com/microphones/Violet/Flamingo-Junior">Violet Design Ltd. Flamingo Junior</a> is also known as: Junior, Flamingo-Jr, Flamingo Jr.</p>
